  <\abstract>
    In this paper, we address improving the cyclic-core processing piece of
    the standard branch-and-bound minimum cover finding algorithm, as applied
    to covering problems, like two-level logic minimization, which can
    represented as a finite abstract cell complex. It is proven that by
    extending the covering table so that it also represents the implicitly
    defined combinatorial finite abstract cell complex of a covering problem,
    any resulting cyclic-core covering problems, with collapsible cells, can
    be exactly solved using only one recursive call instead of two. \ The new
    covering table operation, Collapse, arises from the concepts of cell
    collapse and expansion from topology's simple homotopy theory. \ A
    collapsible cell of the cyclic core allows the two recursive calls
    (cell:in cover and \ out of cover) to be encoded into one call. \ This
    reduces the number of recursive computations needed to solve the minimal
    covering problem. \ Connections to Unate Covering Problems(UCP), Binate
    Covering Problems (BCP), and MaxCostSAT are given.\ 
  </abstract>

  \;

